<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Land Chenla</h1><p class="st-meta">707 CE – 802 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern, inland half of the Khmer kingdom of Chenla after its split in the early 8th century, known only from Chinese records.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chinese sources report that around 707 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chenla">Chenla</a> divided into two parts: a northern Land Chenla, Lu Zhenla, also called Wendan, which lay in the uplands of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and north-east <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a>, and a southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/water-chenla">Water Chenla</a> nearer the delta.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Land <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chenla">Chenla</a> appears more stable and unified than its southern counterpart. It sent embassies to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> court in the 8th century, including one led by a royal heir in 753 who joined Tang forces against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanzhao">Nanzhao</a>. Its people were Khmer-speaking, and Shaivite temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscriptions of the period are found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phu">Wat Phu</a> and Mun river region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Land Chenla's fate after the late 8th century is unclear. Its territories were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> proclaimed his rule in 802, and many historians doubt that the Chinese division matched clear political boundaries.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 707 CE – 802 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:73.77%;width:1.89%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>707 CE – 802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism), Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Khmer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>, <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Upper Chenla, Wendan, Lục Chân Lạp</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=754&amp;focus=land-chenla" aria-label="Land Chenla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7450 1458 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Land Chenla lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7886 2003h0M7846 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7770 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7770 1714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Laos. <a href="/atlas?year=754&amp;focus=land-chenla">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
